# The local judge on the gaming PC (A65). No secrets here: the API key lives only in ~/.config/llm/secret.env on the PC
# (VLLM_API_KEY, chmod 600) and in the Mac's git-ignored .env (LOCAL_LLM_API_KEY).
# JUDGE_MODEL is a placeholder until the blind check against gpt-5.4-mini picks one (A65); clients always ask for
# the served name "judge", so swapping the model is this one line plus `systemctl --user restart llm-judge`.
JUDGE_MODEL=Qwen/Qwen3-14B-AWQ
JUDGE_PORT=8000
# BasedNews judge calls (3,005 with gpt-5.4-mini): ≈ 1,000 tokens in and 174 out on average, at most 4.7 k in and
# 0.9 k out, so 8 k per request is enough. The GPU is otherwise idle (the desktop uses ≈ 0.2 GB): 0.92 of it, and
# prefill in 4 k chunks, leave the most room for parallel requests (KV cache).
JUDGE_GPU_SHARE=0.92
JUDGE_MAX_LEN=8192
JUDGE_BATCH_TOKENS=4096
# FlashInfer's sampler compiles itself with nvcc, which the PC does not have (the CUDA toolkit needs root); vLLM's own
# sampler gives the same sampling without a compiler
VLLM_USE_FLASHINFER_SAMPLER=0
# model-specific flags: Qwen3 writes its reasoning in <think> tags; the parser moves it out of the answer, so the strict
# JSON schema applies to the answer only (thinking off: harmless). Structured output allows no free whitespace: with
# it, 27 of 453 thinking answers looped on blank lines until the token limit (E9 harness 1).
JUDGE_EXTRA_ARGS=--reasoning-parser qwen3 --structured-outputs-config.backend xgrammar --structured-outputs-config.disable_any_whitespace true
